# PERFORMANCE & OPTIMIZATION FOR PYTHON 3.12

## Performance Optimization

- Cache pure functions with `functools.cache` (unbounded) or `lru_cache` where hit rates are high.
- Minimize allocations in hot paths; reuse buffers and precompile regexes with `re.compile()` when needed.
- Move tight numeric loops to vectorized libraries when available, or to C extensions only when justified.
- Avoid premature micro-optimizations; measure with realistic inputs using cProfile or perf_counter() before and after changes.
- Use `__slots__` in classes that will have many instances to reduce memory overhead and improve attribute access speed.
- Use `str.join()` instead of concatenating strings with `+` in loops for better performance.
- Prefer list comprehensions over `map()`/`filter()` for simple transformations as they're more readable and often faster.
- Use `set` operations (intersection, union, difference) instead of loops for set-like operations on collections.

## Memory & Resources

- Use `weakref` for caching or observer patterns to prevent memory leaks from circular references.
- Leverage `__del__` sparingly and prefer context managers for resource cleanup.
- Use `sys.getsizeof()` and memory profilers to identify memory-heavy objects in performance-critical code.
- Prefer generators and iterators over materializing large sequences into memory.